#import "@local/fgbooks:0.14.0": speech, stanza

== THE FOURTH STAGE

Then he began to go forward; but Discretion, Piety, Charity, and Prudence would go with him down to the foot of the hill. So they went on together, going over their former conversations, till they came to go down the hill. Then Christian said, As it was difficult coming up, so, as far as I can see, it is dangerous going down. Yes, said Prudence, so it is; for it is a hard matter for a man to go down into the valley of Humiliation, as you are now doing, and to make no slip on the way; therefore, they said, we have come out to go with you down the hill. So he began to go down, but very warily; yet he caught a slip or two.

Then I saw in my dream, that these good companions, when Christian had got down to the bottom of the hill, gave him a loaf of bread, a bottle of wine, and a cluster of raisins; and then he went on his way,

#quote[“Whilst Christian is among his godly friends, \
Their golden mouths make him sufficient mends \
For all his griefs; and when they let him go, \
He’s clad with northern steel from top to toe.”]

But now, in this valley of Humiliation, poor Christian was hard put to it; for he had gone only a little way before he caught sight of a foul fiend coming over the field to meet him: his name is Apollyon. Then Christian began to be afraid, and to turn over in his mind whether to go back or to stand his ground. But he considered again that he had no armor for his back, and therefore thought that to turn his back to him might give him the greater advantage to pierce him with his darts with ease; so he resolved to venture and stand his ground: for, he thought, if I had no more in view than the saving of my life, it would still be the best way to stand.

So he went on, and Apollyon met him. Now the monster was hideous to behold: he was clothed with scales like a fish, and they are his pride; he had wings like a dragon, and feet like a bear, and out of his belly came fire and smoke; and his mouth was like the mouth of a lion. When he had come up to Christian, he looked at him with a disdainful face, and began to question him like this.

#speech[#smallcaps[Apollyon]: Where have you come from, and where are you bound?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: I have come from the city of Destruction, which is the place of all evil, and I am going to the city of Zion.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Apollyon]: By this I perceive you are one of my subjects; for all that country is mine, and I am the prince and god of it. How is it, then, that you have run away from your king? If it were not that I hope you may do me more service, I would strike you to the ground now with one blow.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: I was indeed born in your dominions, but your service was hard, and your wages such as a man could not live on; for the wages of sin is death, Rom 6:23; therefore, when I had come of age, I did as other thoughtful persons do, and looked to see if perhaps I might better myself.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Apollyon]: There is no prince who will lose his subjects so lightly, nor will I lose you yet; but since you complain of your service and wages, be content to go back, and what our country can afford I here promise to give you.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: But I have hired myself out to another, even to the King of princes; and how can I in fairness go back with you?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Apollyon]: In this you have done according to the proverb, “changed a bad for a worse”; but it is common for those who have professed themselves his servants to give him the slip after a while, and return again to me. You do the same, and all shall be well.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: I have given him my faith, and sworn my allegiance to him; how then can I go back from this, and not be hanged as a traitor?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Apollyon]: You did the same to me, and yet I am willing to pass over it all, if you will even now turn again and go back.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: What I promised you was when I was under age; and besides, I count that the Prince under whose banner I now stand is able to absolve me, yes, and to pardon also what I did in complying with you. And besides, O you destroying Apollyon, to speak the truth, I like his service, his wages, his servants, his government, his company, and country, better than yours; therefore stop trying to persuade me further: I am his servant, and I will follow him.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Apollyon]: Consider again, when your blood is cool, what you are likely to meet with in the way that you are going. You know that for the most part his servants come to a bad end, because they are transgressors against me and my ways. How many of them have been put to shameful deaths! And besides, you count his service better than mine; whereas he has never yet come from the place where he is to deliver any who served him out of their enemies’ hands: but as for me, how many times, as all the world very well knows, have I delivered, either by power or by fraud, those who have faithfully served me, from him and his, though they were taken by them! And so I will deliver you.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: His holding back at present from delivering them is on purpose to test their love, whether they will cling to him to the end: and as for the bad end you say they come to, that is most glorious in their account. For as for present deliverance, they do not much expect it; for they wait for their glory; and then they shall have it, when their Prince comes in his own glory and the glory of the angels.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Apollyon]: You have already been unfaithful in your service to him; and how do you think you will receive wages from him?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: In what, O Apollyon, have I been unfaithful to him?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Apollyon]: You fainted at first setting out, when you were almost choked in the gulf of Despond. You attempted wrong ways to be rid of your burden, whereas you should have waited till your Prince had taken it off. You sinfully slept, and lost your choice things. You were also almost persuaded to go back at the sight of the lions. And when you talk of your journey, and of what you have seen and heard, you inwardly desire vainglory in all that you say or do.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: All this is true, and much more which you have left out; but the Prince whom I serve and honor is merciful, and ready to forgive. But besides, these infirmities took hold of me in your country, for there I sucked them in; and I have groaned under them, been sorry for them, and have obtained pardon from my Prince.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Apollyon]: Then Apollyon broke out into a grievous rage, saying, I am an enemy to this Prince; I hate his person, his laws, and people: I have come out on purpose to withstand you.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Apollyon, beware what you do, for I am in the King’s highway, the way of holiness; therefore take heed to yourself.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Apollyon]: Then Apollyon straddled quite over the whole breadth of the way, and said, I am without fear in this matter. Prepare yourself to die; for I swear by my infernal den that you shall go no further: here I will spill your soul. And with that he threw a flaming dart at his breast; but Christian had a shield in his hand, with which he caught it, and so warded off the danger of that.]

Then Christian drew, for he saw it was time to bestir himself; and Apollyon made at him as fast, throwing darts as thick as hail; with which, in spite of all that Christian could do to avoid it, Apollyon wounded him in his head, his hand, and his foot. This made Christian fall back a little: Apollyon therefore followed up his work with all his might, and Christian again took courage, and resisted as manfully as he could. This grievous combat lasted for more than half a day, even till Christian was almost quite spent: for you must know that Christian, because of his wounds, could only grow weaker and weaker.

Then Apollyon, seeing his opportunity, began to close in on Christian, and wrestling with him, gave him a dreadful fall; and with that Christian’s sword flew out of his hand. Then Apollyon said, I am sure of you now: and with that he had almost pressed him to death, so that Christian began to despair of life. But, as God would have it, while Apollyon was fetching his last blow, to make a full end of this good man by it, Christian nimbly reached out his hand for his sword, and caught it, saying, Do not rejoice against me, O my enemy; when I fall, I shall arise, Mic 7:8; and with that he gave him a deadly thrust, which made him fall back, as one who had received his mortal wound. Christian, seeing that, made at him again, saying, No, in all these things we are more than conquerors through Him who loved us. Rom 8:37. And with that Apollyon spread out his dragon wings and sped away, so that Christian saw him no more. Jas 4:7.

In this combat no man can imagine, unless he had seen and heard it as I did, what yelling and hideous roaring Apollyon made all the time of the fight; he spoke like a dragon: and on the other side, what sighs and groans burst from Christian’s heart. I never saw him all the while give so much as one pleasant look, till he perceived he had wounded Apollyon with his two-edged sword; then, indeed, he did smile, and look upward! But it was the most dreadful sight that I ever saw.

So when the battle was over, Christian said, I will here give thanks to him who has delivered me out of the mouth of the lion, to him who helped me against Apollyon. And so he did, saying,

#quote[“Great Beelzebub, the captain of this fiend, \
Designed my ruin; therefore to this end \
He sent him harness’d out; and he, with rage \
That hellish was, did fiercely me engage: \
But blessed Michael helped me, and I, \
By dint of sword, did quickly make him fly: \
Therefore to Him let me give lasting praise, \
And thank and bless his holy name always.”]

Then there came to him a hand with some of the leaves of the tree of life, which Christian took and applied to the wounds that he had received in the battle, and he was healed immediately. He also sat down in that place to eat bread, and to drink from the bottle that was given him a little before: so, being refreshed, he set out on his journey with his sword drawn in his hand; for he said, For all I know, some other enemy may be at hand. But he met with no other attack from Apollyon all the way through this valley.

Now at the end of this valley was another, called the Valley of the Shadow of Death; and Christian had to go through it, because the way to the Celestial City lay through the midst of it. Now, this valley is a very solitary place. The prophet Jeremiah describes it like this: “A wilderness, a land of deserts and pits, a land of drought and of the Shadow of Death, a land where no man” (but a Christian) “travels, and where no man lives.” Jer 2:6.

Now here Christian was worse put to it than in his fight with Apollyon, as you shall see by what follows.

I saw then in my dream, that when Christian had got to the borders of the Shadow of Death, two men met him, children of those who brought up an evil report of the good land, Num 13:32, making haste to go back; to whom Christian spoke as follows.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Where are you going?]

#speech[#smallcaps[The Two Men]: They said, Back, back; and we would have you do so too, if either life or peace is prized by you.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Why, what is the matter? said Christian.]

#speech[#smallcaps[The Two Men]: Matter! they said; we were going that way as you are going, and went as far as we dared: and indeed we were almost past coming back; for if we had gone a little further, we would not have been here to bring the news to you.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: But what have you met with? said Christian.]

#speech[#smallcaps[The Two Men]: Why, we were almost in the Valley of the Shadow of Death, but by good luck we looked ahead of us, and saw the danger before we came to it. Ps 44:19; 107:19.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: But what have you seen? said Christian.]

#speech[#smallcaps[The Two Men]: Seen! Why, the valley itself, which is as dark as pitch: we also saw there the hobgoblins, satyrs, and dragons of the pit: we also heard in that valley a continual howling and yelling, as of a people under unutterable misery, who sat bound there in affliction and irons: and over that valley hang the discouraging clouds of confusion: Death also always spreads his wings over it. In a word, it is dreadful through and through, being utterly without order. Job 3:5; 10:22.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Then, said Christian, I do not yet see, by what you have said, but that this is my way to the desired haven. Ps 44:18, 19; Jer 2:6.]

#speech[#smallcaps[The Two Men]: Let it be your way; we will not choose it for ours.]

So they parted, and Christian went on his way, but still with his sword drawn in his hand, for fear that he should be attacked.

I saw then in my dream, that as far as this valley reached, there was on the right hand a very deep ditch; that ditch is the one into which the blind have led the blind in all ages, and both have miserably perished there. Again, behold, on the left hand there was a very dangerous quagmire, into which, if even a good man falls, he finds no bottom for his foot to stand on: into that quagmire King David once fell, and would no doubt have been smothered in it, had not He who is able plucked him out. Ps 69:14.

The pathway here was also exceedingly narrow, and therefore good Christian was the more put to it; for when he tried, in the dark, to shun the ditch on the one hand, he was ready to tip over into the mire on the other; also, when he tried to escape the mire, without great carefulness he would be ready to fall into the ditch. So he went on, and I heard him sigh bitterly here; for besides the danger mentioned above, the pathway was so dark here that often, when he lifted up his foot to go forward, he did not know where, or on what, he should set it next.

About the middle of this valley I perceived the mouth of hell to be, and it stood close by the wayside. Now, thought Christian, what shall I do? And every now and then the flame and smoke would come out in such abundance, with sparks and hideous noises (things that cared nothing for Christian’s sword, as Apollyon did before), that he was forced to put away his sword, and turn to another weapon, called All-prayer, Eph 6:18; so he cried, in my hearing, O Lord, I beg you, deliver my soul. Ps 116:4. So he went on a great while, yet still the flames would be reaching towards him; he also heard doleful voices, and rushings to and fro, so that at times he thought he would be torn in pieces, or trodden down like mire in the streets. This frightful sight was seen, and these dreadful noises were heard by him, for several miles together; and coming to a place where he thought he heard a company of fiends coming forward to meet him, he stopped, and began to think what he had best do. At times he had half a thought to go back; then again he thought he might be halfway through the valley. He remembered also how he had already overcome many a danger, and that the danger of going back might be much greater than that of going forward. So he resolved to go on; yet the fiends seemed to come nearer and nearer. But when they had come almost up to him, he cried out with a most vehement voice, I will walk in the strength of the Lord God. So they drew back, and came no further.

One thing I would not let slip. I took notice that now poor Christian was so confounded that he did not know his own voice; and this is how I perceived it. Just when he had come opposite the mouth of the burning pit, one of the wicked ones got behind him, and stepped up softly to him, and in a whisper suggested many grievous blasphemies to him, which he truly thought had come from his own mind. This put Christian more to it than anything that he had met with before, even to think that he should now blaspheme Him whom he had loved so much before. Yet if he could have helped it, he would not have done it; but he did not have the discretion either to stop his ears, or to know where these blasphemies came from.

When Christian had traveled in this disconsolate condition for some considerable time, he thought he heard the voice of a man going before him, saying, Though I walk through the Valley of the Shadow of Death, I will fear no evil, for You are with me. Ps 23:4.

Then he was glad, and that for these reasons:

First, because he gathered from this that some who feared God were in this valley as well as himself.

Secondly, because he perceived God was with them, though in that dark and dismal state. And why not, he thought, with me? though because of the hindrance that attends this place, I cannot perceive it. Job 9:11.

Thirdly, because he hoped (if he could overtake them) to have company before long.

So he went on, and called to him who was ahead; but he did not know what to answer, because he also thought himself to be alone. And before long the day broke: then Christian said, “He has turned the shadow of death into the morning.” Amos 5:8.

Now morning having come, he looked back, not out of desire to return, but to see, by the light of the day, what hazards he had gone through in the dark. So he saw more perfectly the ditch that was on the one hand, and the quagmire that was on the other; also how narrow the way was which led between them both. Also he now saw the hobgoblins, and satyrs, and dragons of the pit, but all far off; for after break of day they did not come near; yet they were revealed to him, according to what is written, “He reveals deep things out of darkness, and brings the shadow of death to light.” Job 12:22.

Now Christian was much moved by this deliverance from all the dangers of his solitary way; which dangers, though he feared them much before, he saw more clearly now, because the light of the day made them plain to him. And about this time the sun was rising, and this was another mercy to Christian; for you must note that though the first part of the Valley of the Shadow of Death was dangerous, yet this second part, which he had yet to go, was, if possible, far more dangerous; for from the place where he now stood, even to the end of the valley, the way was all along set so full of snares, traps, nooses, and nets here, and so full of pits, pitfalls, deep holes, and steep drops there, that if it had now been dark, as it was when he came the first part of the way, had he had a thousand souls, they would in all reason have been cast away; but, as I said, just now the sun was rising. Then he said, “His lamp shines upon my head, and by His light I go through darkness.” Job 29:3.

In this light, therefore, he came to the end of the valley. Now I saw in my dream, that at the end of the valley lay blood, bones, ashes, and mangled bodies of men, even of pilgrims who had gone this way formerly; and while I was musing what the reason should be, I caught sight of a cave a little before me, where two giants, Pope and Pagan, dwelt in old times; by whose power and tyranny the men whose bones, blood, ashes, etc., lay there were cruelly put to death. But Christian went by this place without much danger, at which I somewhat wondered; but I have learned since that Pagan has been dead many a day; and as for the other, though he is still alive, he has, because of age, and also of the many sharp skirmishes that he met with in his younger days, grown so frail and stiff in his joints that he can now do little more than sit in his cave’s mouth, grinning at pilgrims as they go by, and biting his nails because he cannot get at them.

So I saw that Christian went on his way; yet, at the sight of the old man who sat at the mouth of the cave, he could not tell what to think, especially because he spoke to him, though he could not go after him, saying, You will never mend, till more of you are burned. But he held his peace, and set a good face on it; and so went by, and caught no hurt. Then Christian sang,

#quote[“O world of wonders, (I can say no less,) \
That I should be preserved in that distress \
That I have met with here! O blessed be \
That hand that from it hath delivered me! \
Dangers in darkness, devils, hell, and sin, \
Did compass me, while I this vale was in; \
Yea, snares, and pits, and traps, and nets did lie \
My path about, that worthless, silly I \
Might have been catch’d, entangled, and cast down; \
But since I live, let Jesus wear the crown.”]
